        "title": "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "8f7deccd-b00e-58b4-a06a-44a78bd3e26d",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/0b/0b77bdde611689095cdea6a3a08ce9828b9e20040283dc0c251250538a5cfba3.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=6t-2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{6t-2}=\\frac{t}{3t-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
